{
  "title": "ERPNext \u2014 Enterprise Resource Planning (ERP) (34,826\u2605)",
  "content": "Project: ERPNext\nDomain: Enterprise Resource Planning (ERP) | Scale: large | Stars: 34,826\nProblem: ERPNext is a free, open-source ERP system that consolidates business operations including accounting, inventory management, manufacturing, HR, CRM, and project management into a single unified platform, eliminating the need for separate software solutions.\nTarget users: Small to medium-sized businesses, Enterprise organizations, Manufacturing companies, Retail and distribution businesses, Healthcare organizations, Project-based service firms\nCore features: Accounting and Financial Management; Inventory and Order Management; Manufacturing and Production Planning; Asset Management; Project Management and Timesheets; CRM and Sales Management; Human Resources Management; Point of Sale\nIntegrations: Frappe Framework REST API, Multiple payment gateways, Banking integrations, Third-party ERP connectors\nTech stack: Vue.js with Frappe UI, TypeScript, JavaScript + Python with Frappe Framework + MariaDB + Docker, Frappe Cloud (managed hosting), self-hosted options\nKey libraries: Frappe Framework, Frappe UI, onscan.js, Vue.js\nArchitecture: monolith\nDesign lessons:\n- Comprehensive domain coverage: ERPNext demonstrates how a monolithic architecture can effectively serve as an all-in-one ERP solution by organizing features into distinct functional modules (accounting, manufacturing, HR, CRM, etc.) while maintaining data consistency\n- Framework-driven development: Built on Frappe Framework, the project shows strong separation between backend (Python) and frontend (Vue/Frappe UI), enabling rapid feature development and UI updates without tight coupling\n- Flexible deployment options: Supports both managed hosting (Frappe Cloud) and self-hosted Docker deployments, with careful packaging of dependencies to reduce setup friction\n- Open-source business model: 100% open-source with GPL-3.0 license, backed by managed hosting service (Frappe Cloud), demonstrating a sustainable open-source strategy with commercial support layer",
